==English==
{{was wotd|2021|June|15}}

===Etymology===
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{compound|en|hood|t1=head covering attached to a larger garment such as a jacket or cloak|wink|t2=to close one’s eyes}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=88268|date=March 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> (< [[C]]16 'to blindfold').

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=88266|date=March 2021}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhʊdwɪŋk/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhʊdˌwɪŋk/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-hoodwink.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{hyphenation|en|hood|wink}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[cover#Verb|cover]] the [[eye#Noun|eyes]] with, or as if with, a [[hood#Noun|hood]]; to [[blindfold#Verb|blindfold]]. {{defdate|from mid 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|book=I|chapter=Of the Force of Imagination|page=40|passage=Some there are, that through feare anticipate the hang-mans hand; as he did, whoſe friends having obtained his pardon, and putting away the cloth wherewith he was '''hood-winkt''', that he might heare it read, was found ſtarke dead vpon the ſcaffold, wounded onely by the ſtroke of imagination.}}
#* {{RQ:Coryat Crudities|chapter=My Obseruations of the Most Glorious, Peerelesse, and Mayden Citie of Venice:{{nb...|I Call It Mayden because It was Neuer Conquered.}}|page=261|lines=9–16|passage=It is the cuſtome of theſe maydes when they walke in the ſtreetes, to couer their faces with their vailes ''verecundiæ cauſâ'' [because of modesty], the ſtuffe being ſo thin and ſlight, that they may eaſily looke through it. For it is made of a pretty ſlender ſilke, and very finely curled: ſo that becauſe ſhe thus '''hoodwinketh''' her ſelfe, you can very ſeldome ſee her face at full when ſhe walketh abroad, though perhaps you earneſtly deſire it, but only a little glimpſe thereof.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}}
## To [[deceive]] [[use#Verb|using]] a [[disguise#Noun|disguise]]; to [[beguile]], [[dupe#Verb|dupe]], [[mislead]].
##: {{syn|en|snow}}
##* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=20|pages=278–279|pageref=279|passage=For, (having many times torne the vaile of modestie) it seemed, for a laste delight, that she delighted in infamy: which often she had used to her husbands shame, filling all mens eares (but his) with reproch; while he ('''hoodwinkt''' with kindnes) [[least|lest]] of all mẽ [men] knew who strake him.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|chapter=Attorney and Client|page=388|passage=[T]o have to do with you, is to have to do with a man of business who is not to be '''hoodwinked'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Reply of Job to the First Speech of Bildad|translator=John Noble Coleman|title=[[w:Book of Job|The Poem of Job: The Most Ancient Book in the Universe: The First Written Revelation which God Vouchsafed to Man.{{nb...|With a Statement of the Country where Job Resided, and an Enumeration of Some of the Lands which Job Gave to His Three Daughters for an Inheritance amidst Their Brethren, and which were Called after Their Own Names. Translated from the Hebrew with Notes Explanatory, Illustrative, and Critical, by the Rev. John Noble Coleman, M.A. Late Incumbent of Ventnor.}}]]|edition=2nd|location=|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} &#91;[[w:Thomas Constable (printer and publisher)|T[homas] and A[rchibald] Constable]] at the {{w|Edinburgh University Press}}&#93; for private circulation|year=1871|section=chapter IX, verse 24|page=27|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8D3Q3BxxmSUC&pg=PA27|oclc=264992400|passage=The earth is given over into the hand of the Wicked One, / Who '''hoodwinketh''' the faces of its judges. / If this be not so, where, who is HE?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Walter Evans-Wentz|W[alter] Y[eeling] Evans-Wentz]]|chapter=The Testimony of Paganism|title=The Fairy-faith in Celtic Countries|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher=Henry Frowde, {{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=1911|section=section III (The Cult of Gods, Spirits, Fairies, and the Dead)|page=435|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Jyoaa2ZN0qYC&pg=PA435|oclc=30416129|passage=[L]ocal prophecy declares on Merlin's authority that when the tree falls Carmarthen will fall with it. Perhaps through an unconscious desire on the part of some patriotic citizens of averting the calamity by inducing the tree-spirit to transfer its abode, or else by otherwise '''hoodwinking''' the tree-spirit into forgetting that Merlin's Oak is dead, a vigorous and now flourishing young oak has been planted so directly beside it that its foliage embraces it.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Russell C. Stroup|title=Native Sons [letter]|editor=[[w:Henry Luce|Henry R[obinson] Luce]]|magazine=[[w:Time (magazine)|Time]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Time Inc.|date=7 November 1955|volume=LXVI|issue=19|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xhk7AQAAIAAJ&pg=RA5-PA10|column=2|issn=0928-8430|oclc=224518090|passage=Can't the New York myth be exploded before Mr. De Sapio [''i.e'', {{w|Carmine DeSapio}}] '''hoodwinks''' Mr. [[w:W. Averell Harriman|[William Averell] Harriman]] and the Democratic Party?}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Parker Tyler|title=Has the Horse’s Mouth a Gold Tooth?|editor=Albert Frankfurter|magazine=w:ARTnews|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Art Foundation Press|month=January|year=1959|volume=57|issue=9|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BkhUAAAAMAAJ&pg=RA9-PA38|column=2|issn=0004-3273|oclc=919935672|passage=Is it funny in the novel [''[[w:The Horse's Mouth|The Horse’s Mouth]]'' (1944) by {{w|Joyce Cary}}] to hear Gulley tell about '''hoodwinking''' his former or prospective rich patrons with absurd pranks?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Linda Colley|chapter=Out of the Caribbean|title=The Ordeal of {{w|Elizabeth Marsh}}: How a Remarkable Woman Crossed Seas and Empires to Become a Part of World History|location=London|publisher=w:Harper Perennial|year=2007|year_published=2008|page=39|isbn=978-0-00-719219-9|passage=As his correspondence with successive aristocratic First Lords of the Admiralty reveals, he was both unctuously deferential in his dealings with his official and social superiors, and capable sometimes of '''hoodwinking''' them.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[hide#Verb|hide]] or [[obscure#Verb|obscure]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=IV|scene=i|page=15|column=2|passage=Good my Lord, giue me thy fauour ſtil, / Be patient, for the prize Ile bring thee [[to|too]] / Shall '''hudwinke''' this miſchance: therefore ſpeake ſoftly, / All's huſht as midnight yet.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|[Thomas Babington Macaulay]]]|title=Art. I. ''Œuvres complétes de [[w:Niccolò Machiavelli|Machiavel]], traduites par'' J. V. Perier. Paris, 1825. [book review]|magazine=[[w:Edinburgh Review|The Edinburgh Review, or Critical Journal]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [T]he heirs of D. Willison, for [[w:Longman|Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown and Green]],{{nb...|London}}; and {{w|Adam Black}},{{nb...|Edinburgh.}}|month=March|year=1827|volume=XLV|issue=XC|page=285|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_rRZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA285|oclc=950902861|passage=The time was not yet come when eloquence was to be gagged, and reason to be '''hoodwinked'''—when the harp of the poet was to be hung on the willows of Arno, and the right hand of the painter to forget its cunning.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete|rare}} To [[close#Verb|close]] the eyes.
#* {{RQ:Milton Animadversions|page=15|passage=[W]herfore haue you ſate ſtill, and comply'd and '''hood-winkt''', till the generall complaints of the Land have ſqeez'd you to a wretched, cold and hollow-hearted confeſſion of ſome Prelaticall riots both in this and other places of your ''Booke''.}}

